<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.13. Series solutions of famous ODEs</title>
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>


  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>The following differential equation is known as the <term>Chebyshev equation</term> (<m>\alpha</m> is a constant):
  <md>
  (1-x^2)y'' - xy' + \alpha^2 y = 0.
  </md></p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="4in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find two linearly independent solutions using our power series
  method.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>\alpha</m> is a nonnegative integer <m>n</m>, explain why there is a
  polynomial solution.  When normalized according to some convention,
  these polynomials are known as <term>Chebyshev</term> polynomials.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.8in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the polynomial solutions when <m>n=0,1,2,3</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>The <term>Laguerre differential equation</term> is
  <md>
  xy'' + (1-x)y' + \lambda y = 0.
  </md></p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="4in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find one solution to this equation by writing out the first five
  terms.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Explain why your solution is a polynomial if <m>\lambda = m</m>, a
  non-negative integer.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The <term>Laguerre polynomial</term> <m>L_m(x)</m> is this
  polynomial normalized so that <m>L_m(0) = 1</m>.
  Find <m>L_0(x)</m>, <m>L_1(x)</m>, <m>L_2(x)</m> and <m>L_3(x)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>The <term>Legendre equation</term> is
  <md>
  (1-x^2)y''-2xy'+\alpha(\alpha+1)y=0
  </md> where <m>\alpha</m> is a constant.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="3.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find two linearly independent solutions of the Legendre equation.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Show that is <m>\alpha</m> is a nonnegative even integer <m>2n</m>, then one
  solution reduces to a polynomial of degree <m>2n</m> (called a <term>Legendre polynomial</term>).  Find the polynomials
  corresponding to <m>\alpha=0,2</m> and <m>4</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Show that if <m>\alpha</m> is a positive odd integer <m>2n+1</m>,
  then one solution reduces to a polynomial of degree <m>2n+1</m>.  Find the
  polynomial solutions with <m>\alpha=1,3</m> and <m>5</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The original purpose of using power series to solve differential equations 
        was to solve Bessel's equation, which came up when trying to solve the wave 
        equation in a disk.  Let's return to that...
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="4in">
      <statement>
        <p>What happens when you <em>try</em> to use power series to solve <term>Bessel's equation of order 1</term>:
  <md>
  x^2y'' + xy' +(x^2 - 1^2)y = 0?
  </md></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <p>
      Well this seems like a problem. And there is a great method to overcome it, which we will discuss
      more next class.  As a very relevant aside...
    </p>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Do you remember how to solve the equation
  <md>
  x^2y'' + 2xy'-3y = 0?
  </md></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
